Several generic string/sequence primitives are very slow in the interpreter,
enough to dominate real workloads (APROPOS, large-system LOAD, sorting).

Measured (v1.4.34, ~3200-symbol image):
- CHAR (string accessor): ~15 us per call.
- STRING< (generic, keyword-arg version at prelude ~7171): ~5 ms per call.
- SEARCH / MISMATCH (generic): ~14 ms per call (substring of a short pattern in
  a ~15-char target).
- SORT (list merge sort): ~3.8 s for 3200 integers with (function <); the merge
  sort is O(n log n) but the per-op cons/funcall overhead is huge; a large SORT
  with a STRING< comparator times out (minutes).

These made APROPOS take ~28 s (worked around in v1.4.35 with a %STRING-CONTAINS-CI
kernel primitive and %STRING-COMPARE-based sort — see slow-apropos.issue), but the
underlying primitives are still slow for everything else.

Directions:
- Fast paths for SIMPLE-STRING in CHAR/SCHAR/STRING</STRING=/etc. (kernel byte
  access, skip the generic dispatch + character-object boxing).
- A less allocation-heavy SORT (or a kernel sort primitive for the common
  list/vector-of-comparable case).
- Consider why the generic SEARCH/MISMATCH constant is so high (keyword parsing,
  per-element funcall of TEST/KEY, ELT dispatch).

Note: two STRING< definitions exist (prelude ~1545 using %STRING-COMPARE, and
~7171 the generic one that overrides it); the fast one is shadowed.
